<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Gettysburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Gettysburg, fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> on 1–3 July 1863, was the largest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. The Union Army of the Potomac under George Meade defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a>'s invasion of the North, ending with the failed Confederate assault known as Pickett's Charge. Some 50,000 men were killed, wounded or missing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-gettysburg.jpg" alt="Battle of Gettysburg" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thure de Thulstrup / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thure_de_Thulstrup_-_L._Prang_and_Co._-_Battle_of_Gettysburg_-_Restoration_by_Adam_Cuerden_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Confederate victory at the Battle of Chancellorsville, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a> decided on a second invasion of the North. It would upset the Union's plans for the summer, might reduce the pressure on the besieged Confederate garrison at Vicksburg, and would let his army live off Northern farms while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> rested. His army of about 72,000 men could also threaten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, Baltimore and Washington and might strengthen the growing peace movement in the North. Lee began to move north from Fredericksburg on 3 June, and on 28 June President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> replaced Major General Joseph Hooker with Major General George Meade as commander of the Army of the Potomac.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 1 July the armies met northwest of Gettysburg. Brigadier General John Buford's Union cavalry delayed the Confederates until infantry arrived, but two large Confederate corps broke the hastily formed Union lines, which fell back to the hills south of the town. On 2 July the Union line stood in a fishhook shape. Lee attacked the Union left, with fierce fighting at Little Round Top, the Wheatfield, Devil's Den and the Peach Orchard, and assaulted Culp's Hill and Cemetery Hill on the right, but the Union held its lines.</p><p>On 3 July around 1 p.m. 150 to 170 Confederate guns opened what was probably the largest bombardment of the war, which did little to the Union position. About 3 p.m. between 10,500 and 12,500 Confederate soldiers advanced three-quarters of a mile towards Cemetery Ridge in the assault known as Pickett's Charge. Union artillery and rifle fire devastated them, and the few who briefly reached the ridge were driven back. On 4 July Lee began his retreat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Union casualties were 23,055, of whom 3,155 were killed, and the Confederates lost 23,231 by one recent count, though many authors give as many as 28,000. Nearly a third of Lee's general officers were killed, wounded or captured. On 4 July the Vicksburg garrison surrendered to Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a>, and news of Gettysburg ended lingering hopes of European recognition of the Confederacy.</p><p>Historians often call Gettysburg the turning point of the war, usually together with Vicksburg, since Lee conducted no more strategic offensives afterwards, though it was not seen as decisive at the time and the war went on for almost two years. On 19 November 1863 Lincoln spoke at the dedication of the Soldiers' National Cemetery, now Gettysburg National Cemetery, in his Gettysburg Address of 271 words.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Gettysburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Civil War of 1861–1865, the deadliest conflict in American history, with between about 620,000 and 750,000 soldiers dead, ended in Union victory over the Confederacy after battles such as <b>Gettysburg</b> in 1863.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A Confederate army band is said to have played it as the survivors of Pickett's Charge at the <b>Battle of Gettysburg</b> came back from their failed assault, and the Rough Riders sang it over their dead after the Battle of Las Guasimas.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nearer-my-god-to-thee">Nearer, My God, to Thee</a></li></ul></section>
